375, 0.0450439453125, 0.005275726318359375, -0.0004000663757324219, -0.0042572021484375, -0.031005859375, 0.0039005279541015625, -0.0251007080078125, -0.01273345947265625, 0.026519775390625, 0.02947998046875, 0.04559326171875, 0.007293701171875, -0.0006413459777832031, 0.004611968994140625, 0.0162200927734375, -0.044708251953125, -0.0178070068359375, -0.01401519775390625, 0.00948333740234375, 0.003917694091796875, -0.024444580078125, 0.01056671142578125, -0.0178070068359375, -0.043304443359375, 0.023956298828125, 0.0014486312866210938, -0.0049285888671875, 0.006561279296875, -0.0028400421142578125, -0.028106689453125, 0.00714874267578125, -0.008636474609375, -0.0229949951171875, 0.0162506103515625, -0.003597259521484375, 0.0372314453125, 0.0137786865234375, -0.045196533203125, 0.0008664131164550781, 0.002147674560546875, 0.0250396728515625, 0.01617431640625, 0.0003597736358642578, -0.0002803802490234375, -0.0232696533203125, -0.0083160400390625, -0.031951904296875, -0.0108642578125, 0.031463623046875, -0.0007596015930175781, -0.005916595458984375, 0.0285186767578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has explored the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in electrical engineering and physics. GA provides a unified mathematical language for representing geometric transformations and relationships, often simplifying the analysis of multidimensional systems and offering new insights into their structure compared to traditional vector or matrix approaches. However, the extension of classical integral transforms, such as the Laplace transform, to multivector-valued functions in GA remains underdeveloped. A rigorous and explicit definition of the Laplace transform in this context is essential for enabling the direct analysis of GA-valued differential equations, which arise in the study of coupled systems, electromagnetic theory, and control problems where geometric quantities (e.g., rotations, bivectors) play a central role.\n\n**Definitions and Notation**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) denote the real geometric (Clifford) algebra of signature \\((p,q)\\), generated by an orthonormal basis \\(\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_{p+q}\\}\\) with \\(e_i^2 = 1\\) for \\(1 \\leq i \\leq p\\) and \\(e_i^2 = -1\\) for \\(p < i \\leq p+q\\). A *multivector* in \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) is a linear combination of basis blades (elements of different grades). A *multivector-valued function* is a function \\(f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), i.e., for each \\(t \\geq 0\\), \\(f(t)\\) is a multivector. The *exponential map* for a multivector \\(A\\) is defined by the power series \\(e^{A} = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{A^k}{k!}\\), but closed forms are generally available only for special cases (e.g., blades or bivectors).\n\n**Function Spaces and Existence**  \nWe define the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions \\(f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) as follows. Let \\(L^1([0, \\infty), \\mathcal{G}(p,q))\\) denote the space of functions for which each real coefficient function (in the basis expansion) is Lebesgue integrable on \\([0, \\infty)\\). That is, if \\(f(t) = \\sum_{A} f_A(t) A\\) (sum over basis blades \\(A\\)), then \\(f_A \\in L^1([0, \\infty), \\mathbb{R})\\) for all \\(A\\). The Laplace transform is defined for all such \\(f\\) for which the integral below converges for some \\(s > s_0\\), where \\(s_0\\) is the abscissa of convergence determined by the growth of \\(f\\).\n\n**Definition of the Laplace Transform in GA**  \nFor \\(f \\in L^1([0, \\infty), \\mathcal{G}(p,q))\\) and real \\(s > s_0\\), we define\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\(s \\in \\mathbb{R}\\) is a real scalar. Here, \\(e^{-st}\\) is the standard scalar exponential, which commutes with all multivectors in the integrand. This ensures that the integral is well-defined, linear, and that the Laplace transform retains classical properties such as linearity and shift invariance. The transform exists and is unique for all \\(f\\) such that \\(\\|f(t)\\|\\) grows at most exponentially as \\(t \\to \\infty\\), i.e., there exist constants \\(M, \\alpha\\) such that \\(\\|f(t)\\| \\leq M e^{\\alpha t}\\).\n\n**Justification for Scalar Restriction and Counterexample**  \nAllowing \\(s\\) to be a general multivector leads to significant mathematical obstacles. In general, for \\(A, B \\in \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), \\(e^{A} B \\neq B e^{A}\\) unless \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) commute. If \\(s\\) is not scalar, the ordering of \\(e^{-st}\\) and \\(f(t)\\) in the integrand affects the result, and the exponential \\(e^{-st}\\) may not be well-defined for arbitrary \\(s\\). For example, let \\(s = e_1\\) in \\(\\mathcal{G}(2,0)\\), and \\(f(t) = e_2\\). Then \\(e^{-e_1 t} e_2 = (\\cosh t - e_1 \\sinh t) e_2 = e_2 \\cosh t - (e_1 e_2) \\sinh t\\), while \\(e_2 e^{-e_1 t} = e_2 (\\cosh t - e_1 \\sinh t) = e_2 \\cosh t - (e_2 e_1) \\sinh t\\). Since \\(e_1 e_2 = -e_2 e_1\\), the two expressions differ by a sign in the bivector term, demonstrating non-commutativity and ambiguity in the definition. Moreover, the lack of associativity in products involving exponentials of general multivectors can lead to ill-defined integrals. As discussed in Ablamowicz & Fauser (2004), closed forms for \\(e^{A}\\) are unavailable for general multivectors, and the exponential map may not preserve invertibility or other desired properties. Thus, we restrict \\(s\\) to real scalars to ensure mathematical consistency and to preserve the essential properties of the Laplace transform.\n\n**Review and Comparison with Prior Work**  \nExtensions of the Laplace transform to hypercomplex algebras have been considered in the literature. Hitzer & Sangwine (2013) define a quaternionic Laplace transform, but their approach is limited to quaternions and relies on the specific algebraic structure of quaternions, which are associative and have a well-behaved exponential map. Brackx et al. (2001) and De Bie et al. (2012) develop Laplace and Fourier transforms in Clifford analysis, but typically restrict the Laplace parameter to scalars and focus on monogenic (Clifford-analytic) functions, thereby avoiding the complications of non-commutative exponentials. Their transforms are often defined only for functions satisfying additional regularity or analyticity conditions, and do not provide a general framework for arbitrary multivector-valued functions. Ablamowicz & Fauser (2004) discuss the exponential map in Clifford algebras, highlighting the lack of closed forms for general multivectors and the computational complexity in higher dimensions. None of these works provide an explicit, general definition of the Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions in low-dimensional geometric algebras, nor do they systematically address the role of the Laplace parameter or the exponential map in this context. Our approach clarifies these issues and provides a precise scope of applicability.\n\n**Scope and Dimensionality**  \nOur results are explicitly developed for geometric algebras with \\(p+q \\leq 5\\). This restriction is crucial for two reasons: (1) In higher dimensions, the number of basis elements grows exponentially (\\(2^{p+q}\\)), making explicit computation of the exponential map and the Laplace transform intractable; (2) Closed-form expressions for the exponential of a general multivector are unavailable beyond low dimensions, as shown in Ablamowicz & Fauser (2004). All explicit calculations and examples in this work rely on this low-dimensional setting. In principle, some theoretical properties (e.g., linearity, existence for sufficiently decaying functions) may extend to higher dimensions, but practical computation and explicit inversion formulas become infeasible.\n\n**Worked Example: Multivector Differential Equation in \\(\\mathcal{G}(2,0)\\)**  \nConsider the first-order differential equation for a multivector-valued function \\(X(t)\\) in \\(\\mathcal{G}(2,0)\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{dX}{dt} + a X(t) + b e_1 X(t) e_2 = F(t),\n\\]\nwhere \\(a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), and \\(F(t)\\) is a given multivector-valued function. The term \\(e_1 X(t) e_2\\) introduces nontrivial GA structure, as \\(e_1\\) and \\(e_2\\) do not commute with general multivectors. Applying the Laplace transform (with scalar parameter \\(s\\)), and using linearity, we obtain\n\\[\ns \\mathcal{L}[X](s) - X(0) + a \\mathcal{L}[X](s) + b e_1 \\mathcal{L}[X](s) e_2 = \\mathcal{L}[F](s).\n\\]\nCollecting terms,\n\\[\n\\left( s + a + b e_1 (\\cdot) e_2 \\right) \\mathcal{L}[X](s) = \\mathcal{L}[F](s) + X(0).\n\\]\nHere, \\(e_1 (\\cdot) e_2\\) denotes the operator acting on multivectors by"}}